  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 915 PM MDT Fri Aug 14 2026\n\nA noticeable decline in available moisture began last night and\ncontinues today. By this evening, PWATs will reach more normal \nvalues for this time of year. Despite a weak jet streak moving \noverhead, CAMs are still showing a down day with regards to \nconvection. There will be some showers, and a few storms, that \ninitiate off the higher terrain but coverage and intensity will \nbe less, if not considerably so, than that seen on Friday. \nDeterministic models are showing almost no convection across the\nCWA while the NBM favors the San Juans and along the higher \nterrain for eastern portions of the CWA. Seems reasonable for \nnow with PoPs of 30 to 50% for showers/storms over those areas. \nSunday onwards, relatively dry conditions move back though the \nodd shower, thanks to daytime heating, can't be ruled out over \nfavorable areas...looking at you San Juans. Previous model runs\nshowed chances increasing for precip midweek onwards but latest\nguidance has now pushed most precip to the Front Range. \nConfidence remains low in the mid to long range forecast. \n\nWe have one more day of pleasant, near normal highs before a\nwarmup kicks in Sunday onwards. By midweek, highs will return to\nanywhere from 5 to 10 degrees above climatological normals.",
